swehouse.c: the Sunshine declination check crashes and returns nothing

swe_houses_armc_ex2() rejects a Sun declination outside -24..24 for the two
Sunshine systems, and the rejection has two defects, one of them a crash in
the shipped library.

First, the message is written with sprintf(serr, ...) and serr is never
checked. Three public entry points pass serr = NULL: swe_houses(),
swe_houses_ex() and swe_houses_armc(). Any of them reaching this line is a
segmentation fault, and reaching it needs nothing exotic:

    #include "swephexp.h"
    int main(void) {
      double cusp[13], ascmc[10];
      return swe_houses(630070.0, 52.5, 13.4, 'I', cusp, ascmc);
    }

      before   dies with a segmentation fault, signal 11
      after    exits with status 255, the -1 return seen from a shell

No contrived input there: 630070.0 is a date near 3000 BC, the Moshier
ephemeris computes the true Sun, and its declination comes out -24.0019,
because the obliquity itself exceeded 24 degrees before about 2800 BC --
the library's own mean obliquity crosses 24 at a date in 2769 BC. The
check rejects the real Sun of every midwinter that far back, by two
thousandths of a degree at this one, and then crashes delivering the
message. Whether -24..24 is the right bound for dates when the obliquity
exceeded 24 degrees is a separate question and is left alone here; this
patch only makes the rejection survivable. The same crash is reachable with
modern dates through swe_houses_armc(), where ascmc[9] is the caller's own
input:

    double cusp[13], ascmc[10];
    ascmc[9] = 90;
    swe_houses_armc(100.0, 45.0, 23.4392911, 'I', cusp, ascmc);

A fourth route reaches the same line through swe_house_pos(): its house
position call passes the 99 sentinel, which recalls the declination a
previous 'I' call left in a static, and if that earlier call was the
rejected kind the recalled value re-fails the check with serr NULL. The
static itself is upstream behaviour this patch leaves alone; the crash
at the end of it is fixed with the others.

Second, this is the one return from swe_houses_armc_ex2() that writes none
of the four output arrays. The direct caller at least gets ERR back beside
its own untouched memory; the sidereal wrappers do worse, subtracting the
ayanamsa from whatever the caller had there and normalising it into 0..360,
so the garbage comes back shaped like a longitude. The same laundering is
described in "fill the Gauquelin sectors the Porphyry fallback leaves
behind", which fixes it for the Porphyry fallback; this return needs its
own line. After this patch the error path writes zeros: cusp[0..ito],
cusp_speed[0..ito] when asked for, ascmc[0..8] and ascmc_speed[0..9].
ascmc[9] is the caller's own input on this path and is left exactly as
passed.

Positions and speeds on every successful call are untouched: the error
path is the only code this patch adds to. The harness's dump mode, run
pristine and with this patch alone over its full 673,816-call sample,
produces byte-for-byte identical files - identical enough that compare
refuses the pair as indistinguishable, which is its guard against
comparing a binary with itself doing exactly its job. cmp on the two
dumps shows zero differing bytes.

Independent of every other patch in the set; applies before or after any of
them. The ayanamsa rate patch defends itself against this same return
separately, by zeroing the speed arrays before the call, and the two
compose in either order with either or both fixes doing the work.
---
 swehouse.c | 22 +++++++++++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 21 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)
diff --git a/swehouse.c b/swehouse.c
index 1c5bbd3..2b803a9 100644
--- a/swehouse.c
+++ b/swehouse.c
@@ -654,7 +654,27 @@ int CALL_CONV swe_houses_armc_ex2(
       saved_sundec = h.sundec;
     }
     if (h.sundec < -24 || h.sundec > 24) {
-      sprintf(serr, "House system I (Sunshine) needs valid Sun declination in ascmc[9]");
+      if (serr != NULL)
+        sprintf(serr, "House system I (Sunshine) needs valid Sun declination in ascmc[9]");
+      /* Every other return from this function writes the caller's arrays;
+       * this one returned with all four untouched, and the sidereal wrappers
+       * then subtract the ayanamsa from whatever the caller had there and
+       * hand it back through swe_degnorm() looking like a longitude. Zeros
+       * instead. ascmc[9] is the caller's own input and is left as passed. */
+      cusp[0] = 0;
+      for (i = 1; i <= ito; i++)
+        cusp[i] = 0;
+      if (cusp_speed != NULL) {
+        cusp_speed[0] = 0;
+        for (i = 1; i <= ito; i++)
+          cusp_speed[i] = 0;
+      }
+      for (i = 0; i < 9; i++)
+        ascmc[i] = 0;
+      if (ascmc_speed != NULL) {
+        for (i = 0; i < 10; i++)
+          ascmc_speed[i] = 0;
+      }
       return ERR;
     }
   }
